Diagnosis complete. Here is the root cause and a concrete minimal fix.

## Root cause

Three facts in `/opt/nxeon/worker/migrate-real.ts` combine to produce `network soulpilates_internal declared as external, but could not be found`:

**1. The bring-up loop has no ordering and no network pre-creation.**
`/opt/nxeon/worker/migrate-real.ts:5363-5418` iterates `plan.composeDirs` in plain array order and runs `docker compose … up -d` per project (the `up` call is at **line 5388**). The order is just scan order — assembled at **line 858** (`[...new Set([...(items.composeDirs ?? []), ...composeFromScan])]`), which is arbitrary. There is no topological pass over cross-project network references.

**2. The engine never pre-creates *compose* external networks.** The only `docker network create` for anything is at **line 5511**, and it is inside the *standalone* (non-Compose) container path — it uses `p.network` from a container inspect, not from any compose file. Compose projects get nothing. A network declared `external: true` is, by definition, one that compose will **not** create; it must already exist. Nothing in the compose path guarantees that.

**3. No project name is ever pinned, so the external name is order/name-fragile.** A `grep` for `COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME`, `-p `, `--project-name` returns nothing — the engine never sets a project name. Compose therefore derives it from the directory basename, and a normal network keyed `internal` in project `soulpilates` materialises as `soulpilates_internal`, which is exactly what `/opt/mailserver`'s compose references as external.

**The decisive evidence from the failing run** (`cmsciv67q0005hlhwo5vzzu7h`): the four projects were brought up as soulpilates → soulpilates-staging → studionexis → mailserver. `soulpilates` came up first with **3 services running**, yet `mailserver` (last) still could not find `soulpilates_internal`. That means `soulpilates` did **not** create a network literally named `soulpilates_internal` — its own network key is different, or `soulpilates_internal` was an out-of-band network on the source (created by hand, or by a project not in the migration set). So **ordering alone is not sufficient**: the referenced network must be explicitly ensured to exist under its exact external name, because no migrated project reproduces it.

(The other two failures in that run are unrelated to this bug: `soulpilates-staging` = duplicate container name `soulpilates-mongo`; `studionexis` = `pull access denied` for a private image.)

## Concrete minimal fix

Add a **pre-pass before the loop** (insert between **line 5364 and line 5365**, i.e. right after the `Bringing up N project(s)` report and before `for (const dir of plan.composeDirs)`) that, across *all* composeDirs, (i) collects the external networks each project references, (ii) collects the normal networks each project will itself create, and (iii) idempotently `docker network create`s only the external names that **no migrated project will create** and that aren't Docker built-ins. This fixes the orphan-external case (the actual bug) without risking the label-mismatch collision that blind pre-creation would cause when another migrated project legitimately owns that network.

Reading the networks does **not** need `docker compose config` — that would force full variable resolution, which the missing-secrets gate (lines 5371-5383) shows is often incomplete, and would abort. The compose files are already on the target (copied in step 6), so a YAML scan of each file's top-level `networks:` block on the target is enough and needs no env vars.

Sketch (uses existing helpers `sh`, `clean`, `report`, `push`, and `BUILTIN_NETWORKS` at line 352; reuses the same compose-file detection already in `composeEnvFor`):

```ts
// --- Pre-pass: ensure cross-project external compose networks exist ---
// A network declared `external: true` is never created by compose; if the
// project that would create it (under its own <project>_<key> name) isn't in
// this migration set, compose aborts ("declared as external, but could not be
// found"). Create only genuinely-orphan externals, idempotently.
const externalRefs = new Set<string>();   // names referenced as external
const willCreate = new Set<string>();      // names some project WILL create
for (const dir of plan.composeDirs) {
  const cf = clean((await sh(conn,
    `for f in docker-compose.yml docker-compose.yaml compose.yml compose.yaml docker-compose.prod.yml docker-compose.prod.yaml docker-compose.production.yml compose.prod.yml compose.production.yml docker-compose.override.yml compose.override.yml; do [ -f ${dir}/$f ] && { echo ${dir}/$f; break; }; done`
  )).stdout, 255);
  if (!validPath(cf)) continue;
  const project = dir.replace(/\/+$/, "").split("/").pop()!.replace(/[^a-z0-9]/gi, "").toLowerCase();
  // Walk the top-level `networks:` block. For each 2-space-indented key, decide
  // external (-> use `name:` if given else key) vs normal (-> <project>_<key>).
  const raw = clean((await sh(conn,
    `awk 'BEGIN{inn=0} /^networks:[[:space:]]*$/{inn=1;next} inn&&/^[^[:space:]]/{inn=0} inn{print}' ${cf}`
  )).stdout, 4000);
  let cur = "", ext = false, nameOverride = "";
  const flush = () => {
    if (!cur) return;
    const nm = (nameOverride || (ext ? cur : `${project}_${cur}`)).toLowerCase();
    if (!BUILTIN_NETWORKS.has(nm)) (ext ? externalRefs : willCreate).add(nm);
  };
  for (const line of raw.split("\n")) {
    const key = line.match(/^  ([A-Za-z0-9._-]+):/);
    if (key) { flush(); cur = key[1]; ext = false; nameOverride = ""; continue; }
    if (/^\s+external:\s*(true|"true"|'true')\s*$/.test(line)) ext = true;
    const n = line.match(/^\s+name:\s*["']?([A-Za-z0-9._-]+)["']?\s*$/);
    if (n) nameOverride = n[1];
  }
  flush();
}
for (const net of externalRefs) {
  if (willCreate.has(net)) continue; // a migrated project owns it — let compose make it
  const made = await shOk(conn,
    `docker network inspect ${net} >/dev/null 2>&1 || docker network create ${net} >/dev/null 2>&1`);
  if (made) await report(74, "Starting containers…", `  Ensured shared network ${net} exists.`);
}
```

Notes on correctness and safety:

- **Idempotent / non-destructive:** the `inspect || create` guard means if the network already exists (created out-of-band, or on a re-run) nothing changes. It only touches networks; the honest missing-secrets gate at lines 5371-5383 and the running-count verdict at 5405-5416 are untouched.
- **No label collision:** by skipping any name in `willCreate`, we never pre-create a network that a migrated project defines normally (which would otherwise make compose fail with an "incorrect label" mismatch). For the real bug, `soulpilates_internal` is orphan → it gets created; the working `soulpilates` project is unaffected.
- **`external.name` variants:** the awk handles both the modern short form (`external: true`, key = network name) and the `name:` override. Very old compose long-form (`external: { name: … }`) is rare; if you want to cover it, add a match for `^\s+external:\s*$` followed by an indented `name:`.
- **Optional hardening (option b):** if you also want the *defining* project to come up before referencers when both are in the set, sort `plan.composeDirs` so any dir whose `willCreate` set intersects another dir's `externalRefs` sorts first. It's not required to fix this bug (the pre-pass already covers it) but removes the remaining order sensitivity.

